hadiths[count]{hadithnumber,text}:
"260","Narrated to us Ya'qub ibn Sufyan al-Farisi: narrated to us Abu Taubah al-Rabi' ibn Nafi': narrated to us Muhammad ibn al-Muhajir: on the authority of al-'Abbas ibn Salim: on the authority of Abu Salamah: on the authority of Abu Umamah: on the authority of 'Amr ibn 'Anbasah (RA), who said:\n\nI came to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) at the very beginning of his mission while he was in Makkah. At that time, he was concealing his call. I asked him, \"Who are you?\" He replied, \"I am a Prophet.\" I asked, \"And what is a Prophet?\" He said, \"A Messenger of Allah.\" I asked, \"Did Allah send you?\" He replied, \"Yes.\" I asked, \"With what did He send you?\" He said, \"With the command to worship Allah alone, to break the idols, to sever the ties of the idolaters, and to uphold the ties of kinship.\"\n\nI said, \"Excellent is what you have been sent with!\" Then I asked, \"Who will follow you in this?\" He said, \"A freed slave and a free man,\" meaning Abu Bakr (RA) and Bilal (RA).\n\n'Amr (RA) used to say, \"I saw myself as one-fourth of Islam or the fourth of Islam.\"\n\nHe further said, \"I embraced Islam. Then I asked, 'Shall I follow you, O Messenger of Allah?' He replied, 'No, but return to your people. When you are informed that I have left (Makkah), then follow me.'\"\n\nSo I returned to my people and kept awaiting news of his departure. Then a group from Yathrib (Madinah) came. I met them and asked them about the news. They said, \"The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) has indeed left Makkah for Madinah.\"\n\nI said, \"Has he already reached it?\" They replied, \"Yes.\"\n\nSo I set out until I came to him. I said, \"Do you recognize me, O Messenger of Allah?\" He replied, \"Yes, you are the man who came to me in Makkah.\"\n\nI then sought an opportunity to be alone with him. When he was alone, I said, \"O Messenger of Allah, teach me something that Allah has taught you and of which I am ignorant.\"\n\nHe said, \"Ask whatever you wish.\"\n\nI asked, \"Which part of the night is best for hearing (the remembrance of Allah)?\" He replied, \"The last third of the night. Pray as much as you wish, for the prayer is witnessed and recorded until you pray the Fajr prayer. Then refrain until the sun rises and rises to the height of a spear or two, for it rises between the two horns of the devil, and the polytheists prostrate to it. Then pray as much as you wish, for the prayer is witnessed and recorded until the shadow of a spear becomes equal (to its length). Then refrain, for Hellfire is stoked and its gates are opened. When the sun declines, pray as much as you wish, for the prayer is witnessed and recorded until you pray the 'Asr prayer. Then refrain until the sun sets, for it sets between the two horns of the devil, and the polytheists prostrate to it.\"\n\nHe also said, \"When you perform wudu', wash your hands well, for when you wash your hands, your sins depart from the tips of your fingers. When you wash your face, your sins depart from your face. When you rinse your mouth and nose, your sins depart from your nostrils. When you wash your arms, your sins depart from your arms. When you wipe your head, your sins depart from the tips of your hair. When you wash your feet, your sins depart from your feet. If you remain seated in your place after wudu', that is your share of the wudu'. Then if you stand, remember your Lord, praise Him, and pray two rak'ahs facing Him with your heart, and your sins will be as they were on the day your mother gave birth to you.\"\n\n'Amr (RA) said: I said to him, \"O 'Amr, know what you are saying, for you are uttering something tremendous.\" He replied, \"By Allah, my age has advanced, my term has drawn near, and I am free from the need to lie. Even if I had heard it from the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) only once or twice, I would not have narrated it. But I have heard it more than that, as Abu Salamah narrated to me from Abu Umamah (RA), except that I may have erred in something unintentionally, so I seek Allah's forgiveness and repent to Him.\"\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
